---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { DIMENSION_KEYS } from '../lib/types';
import { DIMENSION_META } from '../lib/dimensions';
import { BUILD_META } from '../lib/data';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="How HotelAreaScore computes its six surroundings scores, what the confidence rating means, and what these scores are not."
>
  <h1>Methodology</h1>

  <p>
    Every score on this site is computed automatically from open geospatial data — currently{' '}
    <a href="https://overturemaps.org/" rel="noopener">Overture Maps</a> (release <code>{BUILD_META.release}</code>).
    Nothing here is a review, a rating from guests, or an editorial opinion. There is no way to pay
    to change a score.
  </p>

  <h2>The six dimensions</h2>
  <p>We deliberately show six separate scores rather than one blended number, so trade-offs stay visible.</p>
  <dl>
    {
      DIMENSION_KEYS.map((dim) => (
        <div class="dim-entry">
          <dt>{DIMENSION_META[dim].label}</dt>
          <dd>{DIMENSION_META[dim].description}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>

  <h2>How a score is computed (in plain terms)</h2>
  <p>
    For most dimensions, every relevant nearby place counts toward the score, but places further away
    count for less — a cafe 50 m away matters more than one 500 m away. We combine that with how the
    hotel compares to other hotels in the same city, so a score reflects both an absolute standard and
    the local context. The quiet-surroundings score works in reverse: it starts high and loses points
    for nearby major roads, rail lines, nightlife venues, and airports.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Every constant in this formula — how quickly "further away" stops counting, how much weight
    the city comparison gets — is an explicit first version, not a scientifically validated model.</strong>
    We run a calibration pass against a labeled reference set of hotels before treating any of it as final.
  </p>
  <p>
    The quiet-surroundings score in particular is a deliberate <strong>proxy</strong>, not a validated
    measurement: checked against an independent judgment of how exposed each location actually is, it
    points the right direction but only moderately — it is useful for a rough comparison between hotels,
    not a precise ranking of quietness.
  </p>

  <h2>Confidence</h2>
  <p>
    Each hotel also gets a confidence rating — <strong>High</strong> (≥ 80), <strong>Medium</strong>{' '}
    (60–79), or <strong>Low</strong> (&lt; 60) — reflecting how certain we are in the underlying data:
    the source's own confidence in that hotel's identity and location, how much nearby data we found for
    each dimension, how many hotels we've scored in that city so far, and whether the hotel record
    required merging duplicate entries. A low confidence rating means treat the score as a rough
    indication, not a precise measurement.
  </p>

  <h2>Traveler types</h2>
  <p>
    The persona selector on each hotel page changes how the six scores are <em>weighted and presented</em>{' '}
    for a traveler type (for example, a "quiet sleep" traveler weighs the quiet-surroundings score far
    more heavily). It never changes the underlying facts or scores themselves.
  </p>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Not a safety or crime rating.</li>
    <li>Not a measured, in-room noise level — the quiet-surroundings score estimates environmental
      exposure from nearby infrastructure and activity only.</li>
    <li>Not a price comparison or booking engine.</li>
    <li>Not based on guest reviews.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="disclosure">
    Scores are calculated automatically from geospatial data. They describe the surrounding area, not
    hotel rooms or service quality.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  h1 { margin-top: 24px; }
  h2 { margin-top: 36px; }
  dl { margin: 0; }
  .dim-entry { margin-bottom: 18px; }
  dt { font-weight: 700; }
  dd { margin: 4px 0 0; color: var(--ink-muted); }
  .disclosure {
    margin-top: 40px;
    padding: 16px;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    background: var(--surface);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }
</style>
